NAME
    find - recursive file listing

SYNOPSIS
    find [PATH]

DESCRIPTION
    Walks the directory tree rooted at PATH (default: current
    directory) depth-first and prints every entry (files and dirs)
    one per line. Output includes PATH itself, matching POSIX
    behavior.

    The recursion uses sys_readdir for iteration and sys_fs_size to
    distinguish files from directories — sys_fs_size returns -1 for
    any non-regular entry (same convention ls's JSONL mode uses for
    its "type" field).

    Depth is capped at 32 levels; the FS uses a single uint8_t for
    parent_dir so any tree it can hold is well within that limit.

EXAMPLES
    find                        # walk current directory
    find /                      # walk the whole tree
    find /etc                   # subtree under /etc

EXIT STATUS
    0   walk completed
    1   PATH could not be opened (no recursion happens)

SEE ALSO
    ls(1), grep(1)

NOTES
    No predicates yet — no -name PATTERN, -type d, -maxdepth N, or
    -exec CMD. Filter via pipes: `find / | grep \.json$` is the
    current way to do "find all .json files". Added in session 83.
